TY  - RPRT
TI  - AI Personality Orchestration (AIPO): An Adaptive Framework for Multi-Personality Coordination, Cognitive Role Specialization, and Human-Centered Interaction in Large Language Models
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) increasingly exhibit diverse interaction characteristics, including cautious, direct, expressive, analytical, exploratory, critical, empathetic, and execution-oriented behaviors. Although these characteristics are frequently described as AI personalities, existing AI development paradigms predominantly emphasize model capability, task specialization, instruction following, and output optimization, while providing comparatively limited attention to the systematic orchestration of interaction personalities as an adaptive computational resource. This paper proposes AI Personality Orchestration (AIPO), a conceptual and methodological framework that treats AI interaction personality as a configurable, composable, and dynamically orchestrated system variable. Unlike conventional approaches that assign a relatively fixed persona to an AI agent, AIPO introduces a task-sensitive orchestration mechanism that selects, combines, coordinates, and adjusts interaction personalities according to task requirements, uncertainty, user preferences, interaction context, and resource constraints. The framework distinguishes two fundamental dimensions: cognitive capability and interaction personality. Cognitive capability describes what an AI system can accomplish, including reasoning, knowledge retrieval, planning, verification, and problem-solving. Interaction personality describes how an AI system approaches and communicates with users, including caution, directness, expressiveness, exploratory orientation, critical orientation, empathy, and execution initiative. This separation establishes the possibility of combining different interaction personalities without assuming that personality differences necessarily imply differences in underlying intelligence or factual reliability. AIPO further introduces a multi-personality collaboration architecture comprising Explorer, Critic, Analyst, Executor, and Verifier agents. These agents perform complementary functions within a coordinated workflow, enabling divergent idea generation, adversarial examination, evidence-based analysis, practical execution, and independent verification. A dynamic orchestration controller determines the appropriate personality configuration and collaboration structure for each task. The proposed framework has several potentially important advantages: adaptive interaction, complementary problem-solving strategies, reduced dependence on a single interaction style, improved coverage of complex tasks, personalized human-AI collaboration, and more explicit management of uncertainty and disagreement. It also introduces new research opportunities concerning personality diversity, coordination efficiency, user trust calibration, computational cost, and the relationship between interaction personality and task performance. Importantly, AIPO does not assume that personality diversity automatically improves intelligence or accuracy. Its effectiveness remains an empirical question requiring controlled evaluation against fixed-personality, single-agent, and conventional multi-agent baselines. The paper establishes a conceptual architecture, mathematical formulation, operational mechanisms, research hypotheses, and an experimental methodology for investigating this emerging direction.
