TY  - RPRT
TI  - Chatbots as a source of investment information
AB  - Background and purpose Generative artificial intelligence (AI) chatbots are becoming increasingly accessible sources of information across a wide range of domains, including personal finance and investment. Their ability to provide immediate, conversational, and seemingly personalized responses may influence how individuals seek, interpret, and use financial information. However, the role of these tools in investors’ information-seeking practices and financial decision-making remains insufficiently understood. The present study aims to investigate the use of generative AI chatbots as sources of investment-related information. Specifically, it seeks to describe investors’ use of chatbots, examine the reasons underlying their use or non-use, and explore how chatbot use relates to investors’ characteristics, investment experience, perceived financial risk, and preferred sources of financial information. Study design and methodology The study will use a cross-sectional, quantitative design based on an online questionnaire. The questionnaire will collect information about participants’ age, investment experience, primary investment focus (including cryptocurrencies and other asset classes), and main sources of financial information. Participation will be voluntary. The study will not involve any actual investment activity or manipulation of participants’ financial decisions. The study is intended primarily to provide a descriptive account of current practices and an initial exploration of factors associated with the use of generative AI chatbots for investment-related information. The specific statistical procedures will be selected in accordance with the nature of the collected variables and the final analysis plan. Expected outcomes and significance The study is expected to provide an initial overview of the extent to which investors use generative AI chatbots to obtain investment-related information, the reasons motivating this use, and the characteristics of individuals who report using these tools. It may also help identify differences in information-seeking practices according to investment experience, investment focus, and perceived risk. The findings may contribute to a better understanding of the emerging role of conversational AI in financial information-seeking and decision-making. They may also help identify questions for future research concerning the perceived usefulness of AI-generated financial information, investors’ reliance on these tools, and the potential implications for financial literacy and risk awareness.
