TY  - RPRT
TI  - Can ChatGPT Improve Financial Literacy? An Empirical Investigation
AB  - Traditional financial education experiments, with interventions such as courses or workshops, generally improve the financial knowledge of non-expert subjects but have shown limited impact on their financial behaviors, often with effects that diminish over time. The recent boom of large language models (LLMs) like ChatGPT gives an opportunity to offer personalized financial education. We assess whether different learning methods, including interactive engagement with LLMs, are effective as educational interventions on financial literacy through an experiment with four different interventions. The first one involves the interaction with GPTproxy, a chatbot designed to serve as a proxy of the ChatGPT experience. The second intervention is the interaction with FinTutor, another chatbot instructed to behave like a financial tutor and to use dedicated materials as its primary reference. The third and fourth interventions are, respectively, browsing the internet and reading a passage on each of the relevant topics. In a between-subject design, we split participants into five groups, one for each intervention plus a control group. All groups undergo a financial literacy test and tasks on financial behaviors. After the tasks, the four treated groups go through a “learning phase” in which they can use their assigned learning method to improve their literacy level. Then, all groups re-engage in the previous test and tasks. Lastly, participants complete a set of self-report and demographic questions. All participants complete a Cognitive Reflection Test and a one-item numeracy test. The objective of this study is to determine how the learning methods affect three main dimensions: financial literacy, comprehension of risk diversification, and saving ratio (meaning how much is kept in cash/current bank accounts and not invested in a hypothetical scenario). We aim to assess whether the personalized experience offered by the two chatbots leads to greater improvements by effectively addressing individual knowledge gaps. Additionally, we want to examine whether FinTutor enhances the financial dimensions more effectively than GPTproxy.
