TY  - RPRT
TI  - Factuality and Hallucinations in Large Language Models: A Comprehensive Survey
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) started a new era for artificial intelligence, with skills in language understanding, writing, and thinking. LLMs hallucinate, causing risks in fields they are used, like healthcare to finance and education [Huang et al., 2023]. Hallucinations are believable but untrue text and represent a big block to the trust of AI systems. This study provides a complete and detailed look at the hallucination problem. We offer a strict system that tells apart fact conflicts, lies, and truth errors, moving past simple meanings to show the complex nature of writing failures. We combine ideas from recent papers to examine the cause of hallucinations across the model life, finding main causes in data selection, training goals, and usage methods [Alansari and Luqman, 2025]. We test the best finding methods, comparing black-box guessing with whitebox internal checking, and review fixing methods including Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), self-reflection, and alignment tuning. By comparing the use of auto checkers like SAFE against human notes and studying the moral and meaning effects of the "hallucination" idea, this report aims to serve as a key guide for building trustworthy, fact matched artificial intelligence [Wang et al., 2023].
