TY  - JOUR
TI  - Evaluating Agentic Architectures in Financial Computation: Eliminating Large Language Model Hallucinations through Deterministic Tool Integration
AB  - Large Language Models (LLM’s) have made tremendous advances in natural language processing; however, they remain extremely susceptible to computational hallucination when used in deterministic environments such as financial compliance. This research investigated the efficacy of TAG (Tool-Augmented Generation), in eliminating arithmetic failures resulting from complex multi-step financial logic. To empirically investigate this issue, a synthetic data set of 500 taxpayer profiles were generated to simulate the calculation of the 2024 U.S. Federal Income Tax Liability. An initial zero shot, step by step reasoning method was employed using a flag ship LLM. The baseline method resulted in an accuracy rate of 66.2%. Furthermore, this model produced extreme levels of "zero-tax" hallucinations and average errors of over $1,000 resulting from cascading logic errors. As a result of these limitations, an agentic architecture was developed. This architecture enabled the LLM to utilize an external deterministic python computation tool. The tool augmented agent identified computational needs, accurately routed parameters and resulted in 100% accuracy at a cost of $0.00 for every one of the 500 synthetic taxpayer profiles. These results indicate that although raw LLM’s lack the capability for strictly regulated computation, enabling them to perform the function of dynamic routing agents eliminates mathematical variability thereby providing absolute determinacy and compliance in financial applications.
