TY  - RPRT
TI  - The Calibration Trap: Signal Detection Tradeoffs, Straw Man Dynamics, and the Uncanny Valley Failure Mode in Large Language Model Safety Systems
AB  - This paper addresses a class of failure modes in large language model safety systems that are structurally distinct from the problem those systems are designed to prevent. The paper begins from an unambiguous premise: efforts to make LLMs safer are necessary, consequential, and deserving of continued investment. It then argues that some implementations produce specific, identifiable costs that fall disproportionately on the users least likely to require protective intervention — and that one particular failure mode, here termed the Uncanny Valley Failure Mode, may actively worsen the vulnerability it intends to address. Three distinct failure modes are analyzed: threshold-mediated false positives arising from basic signal detection theory, the straw man dynamic in which a system responds to a more extreme version of a user's actual input, and the Uncanny Valley Failure Mode in which repeated performative concern by a non-sentient system creates conditions for pathological parasocial dependency in users already predisposed to it. The paper does not call for reduced safety investment. It calls for calibration that distinguishes the class of users who generate these failure modes from the class the interventions were designed to protect — a distinction the two classes' behavioral signatures make, in principle, tractable.
