TY  - RPRT
TI  - Lookahead Large Language Models: A Unified Framework for Predictive Token Selection, Reasoning Control, Knowledge Acquisition, and Long-Horizon Language-Model Decision-Making
AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used for complex tasks involving multi-step reasoning, long-context understanding, retrieval-augmented generation, tool use, software development, autonomous agents, and collaborative problem-solving. Although these systems can generate candidate continuations and evaluate intermediate outputs, a decision that appears locally appropriate may lead to poor downstream reasoning, unnecessary computation, factual errors, tool failures, or incomplete task execution. This paper proposes Lookahead Large Language Models (L²M), a unified research framework for predicting the downstream consequences of candidate language-model actions before committing to them. The framework treats token generation, reasoning-step selection, context management, evidence acquisition, tool invocation, memory operations, model routing, and task termination as decision problems. Given a current state and a set of feasible actions, a lookahead controller estimates their future utility, uncertainty, risk, and computational cost, then selects an action according to an explicit decision objective. The framework separates state representation, candidate generation, outcome prediction, decision selection, execution, verification, and experimental recordkeeping. It can be implemented around an existing language model, incorporated into an inference-time controller, or adapted to training and agent orchestration. A modular Research Slot Architecture is introduced to support independent implementation and evaluation. To facilitate systematic research development, this paper presents 300 candidate research directions across fifteen LLM-specific research categories, including token-level generation, reasoning, context processing, retrieval, tool use, memory, multi-agent systems, code intelligence, mathematics, factual reliability, multilingual behavior, personalization, inference efficiency, evaluation, and model training. The catalogue is intended as a structured research roadmap rather than a claim that every direction is novel or effective. The central hypothesis is that explicit prediction of downstream consequences can improve LLM decisions when candidate actions have materially different future effects and the expected benefit justifies the cost of prediction. This hypothesis requires controlled comparisons against fixed, greedy, heuristic, and learned non-lookahead baselines.
